{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q009_heme_bundle_fold_not_homology","answer":"No. SCOPe 2.08 places them in three distinct superfamilies: transmembrane di-heme cytochromes, f.21.1 (SUNID 81342); fumarate-reductase respiratory-complex transmembrane subunits, f.21.2 (81343); and respiratory nitrate reductase 1 gamma chain, f.21.3 (103501). They share fold f.21, Heme-binding four-helical bundle (81344), within class f, Membrane and cell surface proteins and peptides. The fold annotation describes four transmembrane helices arranged as an up-and-down bundle, binding one or two hemes between them. This supports structural similarity, not a single homologous superfamily; the fold’s homology response explicitly says that levels above superfamily do not imply homology.

The annotations preserve important nuances. Within f.21.1, three of four heme ligands are conserved between its two families, with similar but nonidentical heme binding. Within f.21.2, the bundle occurs in one chain in one family and is assembled from two chains in the other. The nitrate-reductase gamma-chain annotation proposes a possible link between the other two superfamilies: correspondence to the gamma subunit of functionally related formate dehydrogenase N, but greater structural similarity to fumarate-reductase FrdC. That suggestion does not merge the superfamilies or establish common ancestry across all three.

Family and superfamily assignments support evolutionary relationships at their respective scopes; shared fold and class do not. Conversely, these separate superfamilies do not prove nonhomology or convergence. A stronger evolutionary claim requires independent sequence/profile evidence, structural alignments comparing topology and equivalent heme-ligating positions, and compatible domain/chain context and phylogenetic evidence. Shared heme binding alone does not justify transferring exact respiratory function or treating the structures as interchangeable templates; those require biochemical and detailed structural evidence.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=heme&limit=30","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/homology"]}]}
